#X obj 230 78 cnv 1 535 356 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -237178 -1 0;
#X text 240 83 Substitutes escaped \\\$0 \, \\\$1 \, \\\$2 \, ... in
the incoming message with the corresponding values of the current patch:
\$0 is the unique patch identifier \, \$1 \, \$2 \, ... are the arguments
passed to the patch. This is useful when a message contains a $‑placeholder
that should be resolved at run time \, not at object creation time.;
#X text 240 199 Typical use cases:;
#X text 250 224 • Building unique names — creating send/receive names
\, array names or file names that are unique per patch \, using \$0
as the patch identifier.;
#X text 250 279 • Parameterized abstractions — resolving \$1 \, \$2
\, ... in messages inside an abstraction \, so that one abstraction
can be reused with different parameters (for example \, different file
names or channel numbers).;
#X text 250 349 • Runtime message construction — building messages
dynamically that contain patch-specific values \, so that the same
patch logic works in different instances without conflicts.;
#X msg 50 482 load file to \\\$0-array @resize;
